gear|What happens electrically when a humbucker is coil-split?|One coil is bypassed|Both coils are doubled|The magnet is removed|The output becomes MIDI|Coil splitting disables one of a humbucker's coils.|DCOIL
gear|Unlike coil splitting, what does coil tapping bypass?|Part of a coil's winding|An entire amplifier|The guitar's bridge|Both pickups at once|Coil tapping uses fewer turns of a pickup winding.|DCOIL
gear|What is a key benefit of wiring a humbucker's coils in parallel rather than splitting it?|Hum cancellation remains|The guitar becomes acoustic|Phantom power is generated|The pickup becomes piezoelectric|Parallel humbucker wiring can sound clearer while still canceling hum.|DCOIL
gear|A traditional P-90 pickup uses which basic coil design?|Single coil|Two stacked humbucking coils|Optical sensor|Piezo crystal|A traditional P-90 is a wide single-coil pickup.|DCOIL
gear|What extra function can a push-pull guitar potentiometer provide?|An electrical switch|An automatic tuner|A built-in speaker|A string winder|A push-pull pot combines a potentiometer with a switch.|DCOIL
gear|Why do many mod-friendly humbuckers have four conductor wires?|To access separate coil connections|To carry four audio channels|To power a display|To drive four speakers|Four-conductor wiring enables splitting and series-parallel modifications.|DCOIL
